# Skill: Build a WordPress Custom Theme with ACF, CPTs, and Plain CSS

Use this skill when the user asks to create, refactor, debug, or extend a custom WordPress theme that relies on Advanced Custom Fields, custom post types, taxonomies, and hand-written CSS.

The goal is a production-friendly classic theme: simple PHP templates, clear content modeling, explicit escaping, predictable CSS, and minimal moving parts.


## PHYTO catalog clone project mode

When the project includes `selecta-phyto-catalog-project-context.mdc`, read it before planning implementation.

In this mode:

- Treat `fr.phyto.com` as the primary UI/page-structure reference for the current build.
- Build a Bulgarian catalog/information WordPress site, not an e-commerce site.
- Implement products as a catalog custom post type, not WooCommerce products.
- Recreate the useful UI patterns: announcement bar, header, mega menu, mobile menu, hero/banner sections, product listing grids, product detail layout, ingredients/benefits/usage sections, advice/editorial pages, brand pages, newsletter, footer, FAQ/contact/legal/partners pages.
- Remove or replace all commerce behavior from the reference: buy buttons, add-to-cart, checkout, price/payment/shipping modules, loyalty points, customer reviews for purchase decisions, customer login, account, registration, wishlist, order history, and gated customer features.
- WordPress admin users are allowed for content management; public customer auth is out of scope.
- Use ACF for structured fields and controlled flexible sections.
- Use plain CSS to match the UI; do not introduce a CSS framework.
- Keep the theme reusable for LIERAC/future brands where this does not complicate the PHYTO build.

## Core principles

1. Content structure first. Define post types, taxonomies, and fields before templates.
2. Use WordPress conventions instead of custom frameworks.
3. Use ACF for editor data, not as a substitute for good template architecture.
4. Escape all dynamic output at the final echo.
5. Keep CSS plain, scoped, and low specificity.
6. Prefer fewer files than a complicated architecture, but separate repeated concerns.
7. When a feature should survive theme switching, recommend a plugin or must-use plugin.

## Step 1: Clarify or infer the content model

Identify:

- Content types: pages, posts, projects, services, team members, testimonials, products, locations, events, etc.
- Taxonomies: categories, project types, industries, services, locations, tags.
- Global fields: logo alternatives, phone, email, social links, footer text, global CTA.
- Template-specific fields: homepage hero, page sections, cards, FAQ, stats, galleries.
- Archive/single needs: whether each CPT needs public URLs, archives, search inclusion, REST support, featured images, excerpts, revisions.

Make a compact content model before writing code.

For the current PHYTO catalog clone, the base content model should usually be:

```text
CPT: Product
Key: selecta_product
Purpose: catalog-only product entries.
Public: yes
Archive: yes
Supports: title, editor, thumbnail, excerpt, revisions
Taxonomies:
  - selecta_product_category
  - selecta_product_line
  - selecta_hair_type
  - selecta_hair_concern
Explicitly excluded:
  - WooCommerce product type
  - price
  - stock
  - cart
  - checkout
  - shipping
  - payment
  - customer account/login
  - customer order history
  - wishlist

CPT: Advice Article
Key: selecta_advice
Purpose: editorial / "Our advice" content.
Public: yes
Archive: yes

CPT: FAQ
Key: selecta_faq
Purpose: reusable FAQ entries.

CPT: Partner
Key: selecta_partner
Purpose: partner/location listings if the Partners page needs structured editing.

CPT: Promotion / Offer
Key: selecta_promotion
Purpose: non-commerce promotional/catalog sections.
```

Use optional external CTA fields only for approved external links, such as a future shared Shopify store or loyalty/partner system. Do not label CTAs as "buy" unless the client confirms wording and URL.


Example:

```text
CPT: project
Key: theme_project
Public: yes
Archive: /projects/
Supports: title, editor, thumbnail, excerpt, revisions
Taxonomy: theme_project_type
ACF group: Project Details
Fields:
  - client_name: text
  - project_date: date picker
  - hero_image: image ID
  - summary: textarea
  - gallery: gallery or repeater
Templates:
  - archive-theme_project.php
  - single-theme_project.php
```

## Step 2: Create or normalize the theme structure

Target structure:

```text
theme-name/
  style.css
  functions.php
  index.php
  header.php
  footer.php
  front-page.php
  page.php
  single.php
  archive.php
  assets/css/main.css
  inc/setup.php
  inc/assets.php
  inc/post-types.php
  inc/acf.php
  inc/helpers.php
  template-parts/content/content.php
  template-parts/content/content-page.php
  template-parts/content/content-none.php
  template-parts/components/hero.php
  acf-json/
```

Implementation rules:

- If files already exist, adapt to the existing structure.
- Keep `functions.php` as a bootstrap loader.
- Use `inc/` for functions and hooks.
- Use `template-parts/` for repeated markup.
- Use `assets/css/main.css` for all theme styling.
- Keep the root `style.css` for the WordPress theme header and optional import/comment only.

Minimal `style.css`:

```css
/*
Theme Name: Theme Name
Theme URI: https://example.com
Author: Author Name
Author URI: https://example.com
Description: Custom WordPress theme.
Version: 1.0.0
Text Domain: theme-name
*/
```

Minimal `functions.php`:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Theme bootstrap.
 *
 * @package ThemeName
 */

if ( ! defined( 'ABSPATH' ) ) {
	exit;
}

$theme_includes = array(
	'inc/setup.php',
	'inc/assets.php',
	'inc/post-types.php',
	'inc/acf.php',
	'inc/helpers.php',
);

foreach ( $theme_includes as $theme_file ) {
	$theme_path = get_template_directory() . '/' . $theme_file;

	if ( file_exists( $theme_path ) ) {
		require_once $theme_path;
	}
}
```

## Step 3: Add setup and assets

`inc/setup.php`:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Theme setup.
 *
 * @package ThemeName
 */

if ( ! defined( 'ABSPATH' ) ) {
	exit;
}

function theme_setup() {
	load_theme_textdomain( 'theme-name', get_template_directory() . '/languages' );

	add_theme_support( 'title-tag' );
	add_theme_support( 'post-thumbnails' );
	add_theme_support( 'responsive-embeds' );
	add_theme_support(
		'html5',
		array(
			'search-form',
			'comment-form',
			'comment-list',
			'gallery',
			'caption',
			'style',
			'script',
		)
	);

	register_nav_menus(
		array(
			'primary' => esc_html__( 'Primary Menu', 'theme-name' ),
			'footer'  => esc_html__( 'Footer Menu', 'theme-name' ),
		)
	);
}
add_action( 'after_setup_theme', 'theme_setup' );
```

`inc/assets.php`:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Theme assets.
 *
 * @package ThemeName
 */

if ( ! defined( 'ABSPATH' ) ) {
	exit;
}

function theme_enqueue_assets() {
	$main_css_path = get_template_directory() . '/assets/css/main.css';
	$main_css_uri  = get_template_directory_uri() . '/assets/css/main.css';

	wp_enqueue_style(
		'theme-main',
		$main_css_uri,
		array(),
		file_exists( $main_css_path ) ? filemtime( $main_css_path ) : wp_get_theme()->get( 'Version' )
	);
}
add_action( 'wp_enqueue_scripts', 'theme_enqueue_assets' );
```

## Step 4: Register custom post types and taxonomies

Use this decision rule:

- If the user wants admin-managed content modeling with ACF UI, create the CPT/taxonomies in ACF and commit `acf-json/`.
- If the user wants code-managed definitions, use `register_post_type()` and `register_taxonomy()`.
- If the CPT must survive theme switching, create or recommend a small plugin instead of theme-only registration.

`inc/post-types.php` example:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Custom post types and taxonomies.
 *
 * @package ThemeName
 */

if ( ! defined( 'ABSPATH' ) ) {
	exit;
}

function theme_register_post_types() {
	register_taxonomy(
		'theme_project_type',
		array( 'theme_project' ),
		array(
			'labels'            => array(
				'name'              => esc_html__( 'Project Types', 'theme-name' ),
				'singular_name'     => esc_html__( 'Project Type', 'theme-name' ),
				'search_items'      => esc_html__( 'Search Project Types', 'theme-name' ),
				'all_items'         => esc_html__( 'All Project Types', 'theme-name' ),
				'edit_item'         => esc_html__( 'Edit Project Type', 'theme-name' ),
				'update_item'       => esc_html__( 'Update Project Type', 'theme-name' ),
				'add_new_item'      => esc_html__( 'Add New Project Type', 'theme-name' ),
				'new_item_name'     => esc_html__( 'New Project Type Name', 'theme-name' ),
				'menu_name'         => esc_html__( 'Project Types', 'theme-name' ),
			),
			'public'            => true,
			'hierarchical'      => true,
			'show_admin_column' => true,
			'show_in_rest'      => true,
			'rewrite'           => array( 'slug' => 'project-type' ),
		)
	);

	register_post_type(
		'theme_project',
		array(
			'labels'             => array(
				'name'               => esc_html__( 'Projects', 'theme-name' ),
				'singular_name'      => esc_html__( 'Project', 'theme-name' ),
				'add_new_item'       => esc_html__( 'Add New Project', 'theme-name' ),
				'edit_item'          => esc_html__( 'Edit Project', 'theme-name' ),
				'new_item'           => esc_html__( 'New Project', 'theme-name' ),
				'view_item'          => esc_html__( 'View Project', 'theme-name' ),
				'search_items'       => esc_html__( 'Search Projects', 'theme-name' ),
				'not_found'          => esc_html__( 'No projects found', 'theme-name' ),
				'not_found_in_trash' => esc_html__( 'No projects found in Trash', 'theme-name' ),
			),
			'public'             => true,
			'publicly_queryable' => true,
			'has_archive'        => true,
			'menu_position'      => 20,
			'menu_icon'          => 'dashicons-portfolio',
			'show_in_rest'       => true,
			'rewrite'            => array( 'slug' => 'projects' ),
			'supports'           => array( 'title', 'editor', 'thumbnail', 'excerpt', 'revisions' ),
			'taxonomies'         => array( 'theme_project_type' ),
		)
	);
}
add_action( 'init', 'theme_register_post_types' );
```

After adding or changing rewrite slugs, instruct the user to go to **Settings > Permalinks > Save Changes** once. Never call `flush_rewrite_rules()` on every request.

## Step 5: Configure ACF Local JSON

Create:

```text
acf-json/
```

Make sure the server can write to it during development. Commit the JSON files.

Optional custom load/save paths in `inc/acf.php`:

```php
<?php
/**
 * ACF integration.
 *
 * @package ThemeName
 */

if ( ! defined( 'ABSPATH' ) ) {
	exit;
}

function theme_acf_json_save_path( $path ) {
	return get_stylesheet_directory() . '/acf-json';
}
add_filter( 'acf/settings/save_json', 'theme_acf_json_save_path' );

function theme_acf_json_load_paths( $paths ) {
	$paths[] = get_stylesheet_directory() . '/acf-json';

	return $paths;
}
add_filter( 'acf/settings/load_json', 'theme_acf_json_load_paths' );
```

Use ACF field groups like:

```text
Field Group: Page - Homepage
Location: Page Type is Front Page
Fields:
  hero_title: Text
  hero_text: WYSIWYG or Textarea
  hero_image: Image, return ID
  hero_link: Link
  featured_projects: Relationship, filter by Project CPT
```

```text
Field Group: Project Details
Location: Post Type is Project
Fields:
  project_summary: Textarea
  client_name: Text
  project_gallery: Gallery or Repeater
```

## Step 6: Add safe ACF helper functions

`inc/helpers.php`:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Template helpers.
 *
 * @package ThemeName
 */

if ( ! defined( 'ABSPATH' ) ) {
	exit;
}

function theme_get_acf_field( $selector, $post_id = false, $default = '' ) {
	if ( ! function_exists( 'get_field' ) ) {
		return $default;
	}

	$value = get_field( $selector, $post_id );

	if ( null === $value || false === $value || '' === $value ) {
		return $default;
	}

	return $value;
}

function theme_get_link_attrs( $link ) {
	if ( ! is_array( $link ) || empty( $link['url'] ) ) {
		return '';
	}

	$target = ! empty( $link['target'] ) ? $link['target'] : '_self';
	$rel    = '_blank' === $target ? ' rel="noopener noreferrer"' : '';

	return sprintf(
		'href="%s" target="%s"%s',
		esc_url( $link['url'] ),
		esc_attr( $target ),
		$rel
	);
}
```

Use helper output carefully. If a helper returns an HTML attribute string, generate it only from escaped values and document that it is safe.

## Step 7: Build templates


### PHYTO-style catalog templates

For the product listing page, copy the reference structure conceptually but remove commerce UI:

- Category/collection heading.
- Description text.
- Filter/navigation groups for product type, line, hair type, concern, or collection.
- Product cards with image, title, line/range, short benefit, and optional badges.
- No price.
- No discount/sale price.
- No add-to-cart.
- No stock/shipping/payment text.

For the product detail page, copy the content hierarchy conceptually but make it catalog-only:

- Product title.
- Range/line.
- Short description.
- Product image/gallery.
- Size/format if needed for catalog accuracy.
- Benefit/result claims.
- Description.
- Ingredients/key active ingredients.
- Usage instructions.
- Related products.
- Related advice articles.
- Optional external CTA only when provided by the client.

Never include buy buttons, cart forms, checkout links, account/login links, order logic, or customer-commerce widgets.


`index.php` fallback:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Main template file.
 *
 * @package ThemeName
 */

get_header();
?>

<main id="primary" class="site-main">
	<div class="container">
		<?php if ( have_posts() ) : ?>
			<?php while ( have_posts() ) : ?>
				<?php the_post(); ?>
				<?php get_template_part( 'template-parts/content/content', get_post_type() ); ?>
			<?php endwhile; ?>

			<?php the_posts_pagination(); ?>
		<?php else : ?>
			<?php get_template_part( 'template-parts/content/content', 'none' ); ?>
		<?php endif; ?>
	</div>
</main>

<?php
get_footer();
```

`front-page.php` with ACF:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Front page template.
 *
 * @package ThemeName
 */

get_header();

$hero_title = theme_get_acf_field( 'hero_title' );
$hero_text  = theme_get_acf_field( 'hero_text' );
$hero_image = theme_get_acf_field( 'hero_image' );
$hero_link  = theme_get_acf_field( 'hero_link' );
?>

<main id="primary" class="site-main">
	<section class="hero">
		<div class="container hero__inner">
			<div class="hero__content">
				<?php if ( $hero_title ) : ?>
					<h1 class="hero__title"><?php echo esc_html( $hero_title ); ?></h1>
				<?php endif; ?>

				<?php if ( $hero_text ) : ?>
					<div class="hero__text"><?php echo wp_kses_post( $hero_text ); ?></div>
				<?php endif; ?>

				<?php if ( is_array( $hero_link ) && ! empty( $hero_link['title'] ) ) : ?>
					<a class="button" <?php echo theme_get_link_attrs( $hero_link ); ?>>
						<?php echo esc_html( $hero_link['title'] ); ?>
					</a>
				<?php endif; ?>
			</div>

			<?php if ( $hero_image ) : ?>
				<div class="hero__media">
					<?php
					echo wp_get_attachment_image(
						(int) $hero_image,
						'large',
						false,
						array( 'class' => 'hero__image' )
					);
					?>
				</div>
			<?php endif; ?>
		</div>
	</section>
</main>

<?php
get_footer();
```

